WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:02.960
If climate change were
an animal or plant,

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:05.520
I think it would be
some sort of parasite,

00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:07.320
because it's the only thing

00:00:07.360 --> 00:00:10.360
that's actually growing
and getting bigger,

00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:13.120
while everything else
starts dying off.

00:00:13.160 --> 00:00:15.320
The environment
is definitely very special

00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:18.800
because if we get rid of it,
there's nowhere else we can go.

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:21.960
It's a very one in a million planet.

00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:29.520
A 10-year-old some years ago
got cross with me

00:00:29.560 --> 00:00:31.440
and said, "You don't get it."

00:00:31.480 --> 00:00:35.280
He said, "You grew up thinking that
polar bears would be there forever."

00:00:35.320 --> 00:00:39.640
He said, "I've grown up
knowing they will go extinct."

00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:41.880
I don't remember
when I first became aware

00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:44.680
about climate change. I don't
remember a time where I didn't know

00:00:44.720 --> 00:00:46.840
what it was. It feels like
it's always been there.

00:00:46.880 --> 00:00:49.080
Children are growing up
with this knowledge

00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:53.680
and this awareness, whereas anyone
over the age of, let's say 30,

00:00:53.720 --> 00:00:56.720
is having to wake up to this
and realise it.

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:09.280
When we asked young people about
how climate change made me feel,

00:01:09.320 --> 00:01:13.520
three-quarters or more,
think the future is frightening.

00:01:13.560 --> 00:01:17.160
So we know that it's having
an emotional impact on children

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:19.400
and young people
all around the world.

00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:23.320
I think climate change
is really scary.

00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:26.520
And the fact that lots of people
aren't doing much about it,

00:01:26.560 --> 00:01:28.800
just sort of freaks me out.

00:01:28.840 --> 00:01:30.200
Eight out of 10 told us

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:32.280
that people had failed to
take care of the planet.

00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:36.360
So this is young people
and children looking to adults,

00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:39.240
looking to older people,
looking to people in power,

00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:42.800
and saying, "Why haven't you
done something about this?"

00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:47.040
It makes me feel angry
to see such a clear issue

00:01:47.080 --> 00:01:50.320
and to see everyone
knowing about this issue,

00:01:50.360 --> 00:01:53.880
and still not doing anything
that they really should be.

00:01:53.920 --> 00:01:55.960
It's just frustrating.

00:01:56.000 --> 00:01:59.840
And I think it's also tiring
to feel so scared and angry.

00:01:59.880 --> 00:02:02.480
One of the worst things
we can do to children

00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:05.480
and young people is say, "Well,
it's your responsibility now."

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:07.080
I've heard this said to children.

00:02:07.120 --> 00:02:09.080
"Your generation will fix this.

00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:13.440
Your generation is so inspiring
and creative and innovative.

00:02:13.480 --> 00:02:15.640
We're going to leave this to you
to fix."

00:02:15.680 --> 00:02:17.560
There is an idea by older people

00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:19.560
that it is on young people
to fix it.

00:02:19.600 --> 00:02:21.160
But I think that's really unfair,

00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:23.880
and I think it's a collective
responsibility,

00:02:23.920 --> 00:02:26.600
not just on young people
and not just on me.

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:29.440
One of
the most worrying statistics

00:02:29.480 --> 00:02:32.480
was that 48% told us
they were dismissed or ignored

00:02:32.520 --> 00:02:35.160
when they tried to talk about
climate change.

00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:37.800
That, we could do something about
this afternoon.

00:02:37.840 --> 00:02:40.560
That, we could do
something about today.

00:02:40.600 --> 00:02:44.760
In places like school, I feel
like they don't really listen,

00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:47.800
and don't really respect the fact
that I've thought about it

00:02:47.840 --> 00:02:50.640
and tried to educate myself
about it.

00:02:50.680 --> 00:02:53.600
I kind of feel really angry
and frustrated,

00:02:53.640 --> 00:02:56.480
because I am actually
saying something meaningful.

00:02:56.520 --> 00:03:00.000
And if you're not prepared
to listen to your children,

00:03:00.040 --> 00:03:03.680
it's going to have a big effect,
because we actually

00:03:03.720 --> 00:03:06.880
do have something to say, and we are
actually trying to make a change.

00:03:06.920 --> 00:03:11.440
My advice to adults,
parents, teachers, is

00:03:11.480 --> 00:03:14.600
do not put it off,
don't push it away.

00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:18.680
Don't save it up and just have
that big conversation.

00:03:18.720 --> 00:03:22.000
Talk about it regularly.
That's crucial.

00:03:22.040 --> 00:03:24.520
And it's OK for you
to say to your children,

00:03:24.560 --> 00:03:26.240
"I don't have all the answers.

00:03:26.280 --> 00:03:29.560
We're not sure exactly
how to deal with all of this.

00:03:29.600 --> 00:03:31.320
But let's find out together."

